S.append(title(
    EYE, 'Connected\ninteractions', 'Networks, protocols and application messages',
    notes='120-minute lecture plus a separate 120-minute tutorial. Theory-first route: 0-8 opening, 8-15 roles/communication, 15-29 layers/links/IP, 29-40 delivery/transport, 40-49 endpoints, 49-60 diagnosis/discussion, 60-70 break, 70-80 application protocols, 80-94 APIs/REST, 94-103 WebSocket excerpts, 103-115 framing/state/failure, 115-120 tutorial handoff. This content supports the later final-course quiz. There is no end-of-lecture quiz today. The detailed Scene implementation belongs to the separate tutorial. Reuse the Week 4 API and Week 7 object knowledge. Students trace messages, distinguish local/shared state and explain what an absent acknowledgement proves. Source: https://github.com/sd5913/pfad/tree/66067e191bd1c58b9c8d530dbc3fa8b50d69657a/week09'))
S.append(question('short_answer', 'Ask me anything about the group project',
    hint='Post one question your team wants to resolve.', eyebrow_text='OPENING / GROUP PROJECT AMA',
    notes='0-5 minutes. Open the existing ClassPoint Short Answer activity so students can post project questions. Invite one question per team or individual, then group related questions and answer the most shared ones. Scope, teamwork, requirements, technical choices and current blockers are all welcome. Keep unresolved project-specific questions for the allocated tutorial support time. Do not invent assessment policy: use the current project brief and tutor-confirmed schedule. If ClassPoint is unavailable, collect the same questions verbally or on paper rather than introducing a new external form. Do not publish private student data or identify students in the public deck. This is an ungraded AMA, not a new quiz.'))
S.append(L.steps('From a gesture to another program', [('Plan', 'Choose one usable increment.'), ('Sense', 'MediaPipe supplies a position.'), ('Send', 'Another process receives an action.')], notes='5-7 minutes. This bridge follows the actual Week 8 material: Agile/Scrum, GitHub tracking, branch/PR/review workflow and MediaPipe hand landmarks. Do not imply that networking replaces this workflow or that every group must add it. A team can divide a sensor producer and a visual receiver across modules, processes or machines, but must agree their message contract and test the integration. Ask students to reuse their next usable increment rather than invent a new networking project. Source: https://github.com/sd5913/teaching/blob/da0e86c67172c765ab5d7de9c5dfe1f625370667/deck/week08.py .', conclusion='Use your existing GitHub PR and project workflow to check the change.'))
S.append(L.steps('Follow one message.', [('Input', 'A fingertip or slider supplies x.'), ('Message', 'Send the intended action.'), ('Application', 'Interpret it and reply.')], notes='7-8 minutes. Establish one coherent motivating example for the theory that follows. A normalized x coordinate could become a bounded set_x action; the tutorial uses a slider as a deterministic stand-in, so camera access is not required. Explain that we will study OSI/TCP-IP, delivery modes, diagnostic commands and advanced API patterns before applying them in the separate tutorial. Networking is useful only if the project needs a boundary between processes or devices. This week the client talks to an application through an API/WebSocket. Next week the application talks to databases and other resources. A network database commonly uses a TCP socket plus its database protocol; the socket is an endpoint, not the protocol itself. Embedded SQLite is a useful exception and does not require a network server. Do not teach database setup here. The final-course quiz later can assess the concepts taught here; there is no end-of-lecture quiz today.', conclusion='Today: client to app. Next week: app to database.'))
S.append(L.diagram("Metcalfe's law", F.network_growth(), notes='8-10 minutes. Introduce Metcalfe\'s law: network value is proposed to grow roughly with the square of the number of users. Use the growing number of possible pairs to motivate that idea. Reintroduce the source lecture network-effects question with a shared drawing or multiplayer installation. For n participants, n(n-1)/2 counts possible undirected pairs if every pair can connect. It does not measure value, traffic or engagement, and real networks need not connect every pair directly. Metcalfe\'s law is a useful hypothesis about network value, not a universal experimentally proven formula. Ask whether an empty chat group becomes useful merely by adding silent accounts. Source crosswalk: supplied 2025 PDF slide 24. The pair count is a combinatorial derivation, not an economic estimate.'))
S.append(L.compare('When do more connections create value?', ('Silent group', '4 people, 6 possible links', 'Nobody shares or responds.'), ('Active group', '4 people, 6 possible links', 'People share, collaborate and help.'), notes='10-11 minutes. Introduce Metcalfe\'s law on the previous slide before qualifying it here. Both groups can have the same number of possible pairwise links but different usefulness. The pair count is exact under the fully connected undirected model; measured economic or creative value is not. Network purpose, participation and costs matter. Keep this qualification after the explanation of the law. Together the two slides take three minutes within the existing 8-15 block.', conclusion='The same number of links can support different amounts of useful activity.'))
S.append(L.compare('Client and server describe roles.', ('Client', 'Starts the exchange', 'A browser requests artwork.'), ('Server', 'Listens and responds', 'A Python app returns the data.'), notes='11-12 minutes. Ask students to identify the initiating process and the listening process in the Week 4 example. A server need not be a large remote computer. Two browser tabs are separate views, and the Python process is a third participant. Peer-to-peer describes a different organization of roles, not the absence of protocols or addresses. Distinguish source files, running processes and physical devices. The same laptop can host the browser and service during development and different machines later.', conclusion='Both programs can run on **one laptop**.'))
S.append(L.rows('Which directions can messages travel?', [('Simplex', 'One direction.'), ('Half duplex', 'Both directions, taking turns.'), ('Full duplex', 'Both directions can overlap.')], notes='12-15 minutes. A broadcast-only display, walkie-talkie conversation and telephone call are useful direction analogies, not claims about their exact network stacks. Full duplex is a capability: an application can still choose a send-then-wait loop. Both sides need compatible formats, ordering and meaning. Reachability also depends on listener binding, routes and firewall policy, not just a valid IP address. A TCP acknowledgement does not prove Scene.apply ran or that a value reached storage. Predict whether a server can receive bytes and crash before replying. Source: RFC 9293 sections 2.2, 3.1 and 3.8, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293 .', conclusion='A **protocol** gives both sides agreed rules.'))
S.append(L.diagram('Seven OSI layers, four Internet layers', F.osi_layers(), notes='15-21 minutes. Walk upward: physical signals, local-link frames, routed IP packets, transport endpoints, then conversation/representation/application responsibilities. Explain each job with the control message: physical encodes bits as signals; data link frames local-hop traffic; network addresses/routes packets across networks; transport connects process endpoints and supplies its chosen delivery service; session organizes dialogues; presentation handles representation/encoding; application defines the service operations. This reference model does not require seven separate libraries. Do not merely recite a mnemonic. The diagram groups session/presentation/application into the Internet application layer and data-link/physical into link. This is an approximate teaching mapping between models, not a rule that every real protocol occupies exactly one box. In our app TCP carries WebSocket frames containing JSON, while loopback avoids a physical cable. TLS is encryption between application and transport, not a reason to claim HTTPS is a new physical layer. Sources: ITU-T X.200, https://www.itu.int/rec/T-REC-X.200-199407-I/en ; RFC 1122 section 1.1.3, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 . Ask which layer needs changing if the same JSON travels over Ethernet instead of Wi-Fi.'))
S.append(L.diagram("Each layer carries the next layer's data", F.encapsulation(), notes='21-23 minutes. Trace one JSON action down the stack: WebSocket frames messages, TCP carries ordered bytes, IP routes packets and the link carries a frame to the next hop. Headers add information for that layer. At the receiver each layer interprets its own header before passing payload upward. A WebSocket message can itself span frames, and TCP can split or combine those bytes across segments; this is a conceptual nesting, not a promise of one message per packet. At a router the link envelope changes while the IP packet continues toward its destination. HTTPS/WSS adds TLS protection; the simplified local ws example omits it. Sources: RFC 1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 ; RFC 6455 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455 . Ask which piece contains set_x and which identifies TCP port 8009.'))
S.append(L.compare('Choose the link for the job.', ('Wired', 'Ethernet\nUSB / sensor buses', 'Connect computers, devices or components.'), ('Wireless', 'Wi-Fi / 5G\nBluetooth / LoRa', 'Different ranges, data rates and power needs.'), notes='23-25 minutes. Retain the historical wired/wireless examples without pretending they have one fixed speed or range. Versions, environment, topology and configuration matter. Ethernet and Wi-Fi commonly carry IP; USB, I2C and 1-Wire are not interchangeable IP network technologies. A sensor bus can feed a gateway that exposes an API. LoRa is a radio modulation technology; LoRaWAN adds networking. Ask whether a room-scale high-rate video stream and a battery-powered distant temperature sensor should make the same link choice. No numerical throughput claims or hardware setup are required. Primary references: IEEE 802 LAN/MAN overview https://www.ieee802.org/ ; Bluetooth technology overview https://www.bluetooth.com/learn-about-bluetooth/tech-overview/ ; LoRa Alliance https://lora-alliance.org/about-lorawan/ .', conclusion='A video stream and a battery sensor need different links.'))
S.append(L.compare('Two ways to write an IP address', ('IPv4', '**192.168.1.23**', '32-bit addresses'), ('IPv6', '**2001:db8::23**', '128-bit addresses'), notes='25-27 minutes. A network interface may have several addresses, and addresses can change. The IPv6 example uses the documentation prefix, not a live destination. For IPv4, a /24 prefix corresponds to 255.255.255.0. DHCP can lease an address and supply configuration such as router and DNS information. IPv6 uses its own mechanisms, including SLAAC and DHCPv6; do not teach all configuration as IPv4 DHCP. A DNS server and a default gateway do different jobs even when a home router provides both. Loopback 127.0.0.1 and ::1 stay on this host. Sources: RFC 791 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc791 ; RFC 8200 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8200 ; RFC 2131 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2131 ; RFC 4862 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4862 .', conclusion='One device can have **several addresses**.'))
S.append(L.rows('IP settings answer different questions.', [('Prefix', 'Which addresses are on my local network?'), ('Gateway', 'Where should off-network packets go next?'), ('DNS resolver', 'Where do I ask about names?')], notes='25-27 minutes. A network interface may have several addresses, and addresses can change. The IPv6 example uses the documentation prefix, not a live destination. For IPv4, a /24 prefix corresponds to 255.255.255.0. DHCP can lease an address and supply configuration such as router and DNS information. IPv6 uses its own mechanisms, including SLAAC and DHCPv6; do not teach all configuration as IPv4 DHCP. A DNS server and a default gateway do different jobs even when a home router provides both. Loopback 127.0.0.1 and ::1 stay on this host. Sources: RFC 791 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc791 ; RFC 8200 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8200 ; RFC 2131 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2131 ; RFC 4862 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4862 .', conclusion='**DHCP** can supply an IPv4 address and these settings.'))
S.append(L.diagram('Same subnet or through the gateway?', F.host_route(), notes='27-29 minutes. Work the illustrated IPv4 /24 example. The local route covers 192.168.1.0/24, so 192.168.1.42 is on-link. A destination outside this local prefix uses the configured default route if no more specific route matches. The packet keeps its final IP destination while the local-link frame goes to the next-hop gateway. Routers choose a next hop; they do not carry one unchanging Ethernet frame across the entire Internet. We simplify to one active interface and one default route. Ask which setting students would inspect when local devices work but off-network destinations fail. Source: RFC 1122 section 3.3.1, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 .'))
S.append(L.diagram('Unicast: one addressed recipient', F.packet_delivery('unicast'), notes='29-31 minutes. The highlighted receiver is one IP destination. Several clients can each have their own unicast connection to a server. The presence of several machines on the same network does not make every packet intended for all of them. Ask which receiver should handle this addressed message. Source: RFC 1122, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 .'))
S.append(L.diagram('Broadcast: all on a local scope', F.packet_delivery('broadcast'), notes='31-33 minutes. Broadcast targets all recipients in a defined broadcast scope, not everyone on the Internet. A local IPv4 broadcast or Ethernet broadcast has a bounded scope; routers generally do not forward ordinary local broadcasts. IPv6 has no broadcast addresses and uses multicast for relevant functions. Distinguish this network operation from a Python function named broadcast that loops over separate connections. Sources: RFC 1122 section 3.3.6, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 ; RFC 4291 section 2, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4291 .'))
S.append(L.diagram('Multicast: members of an addressed group', F.packet_delivery('multicast'), notes='33-35 minutes. A multicast group address identifies participating recipients. Delivery depends on membership, network support and scope; it is not a promise that any arbitrary remote group will receive a packet. Compare unicast to one, broadcast to all within scope and multicast to a group. Our browser WebSocket scene uses one connection per client, not IP multicast. Later broadcast() is application fanout over those separate unicast TCP connections. Source: RFC 1112, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1112 .'))
S.append(L.diagram('TCP starts with a three-way handshake', F.tcp_handshake(), notes='35-38 minutes. A client sends SYN, the listening endpoint returns SYN+ACK, and the client acknowledges. This establishes sequence-number synchronization for a connection; it is not one handshake for every application message. TCP then carries ordered bytes in both directions, with sequence numbers, acknowledgements and retransmission. Loss can delay later bytes and a connection can eventually fail. Do not repeat the source deck claims that TCP is error-immune or always needs three times as many packets. TLS and the WebSocket opening handshake are additional protocols, not these three TCP messages. Source: RFC 9293 section 3.5, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293 .'))
S.append(L.compare('TCP carries bytes. UDP carries datagrams.', ('TCP', 'Ordered byte stream', 'Retransmits missing bytes; waiting can add delay.'), ('UDP', 'Separate datagrams', 'No guarantee of delivery or order.'), notes='38-40 minutes. Reliable means TCP works to deliver an ordered byte stream or reports failure, not that all applications always succeed. UDP preserves a datagram boundary but an application may need sequence numbers, loss handling and congestion control. A video call may prefer timely media to waiting for every obsolete sample; a coin transaction needs careful application semantics even over TCP. UDP does not automatically make an application faster. QUIC is an example of reliable transport built over UDP, so HTTP/3 does not disprove these UDP-layer guarantees. Sources: RFC 9293 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293 ; RFC 768 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc768 ; RFC 9114 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114 .', conclusion='The application still decides what a message means.'))
S.append(L.diagram('One listening port, many connections', F.endpoint_connections(), notes='40-43 minutes. A socket is a communication endpoint used by a program; the application protocol defines the rules and meaning of its messages. TCP and UDP have separate 16-bit port namespaces. A TCP connection is distinguished by its local and remote address/port pairs. The listening server can accept many clients on TCP 8009 while each client uses its own source port. Both HTTP routes and /ws can share this listener because the application dispatches the path/protocol. Conventional ports do not permanently reserve an application identity; here we choose 8009 to avoid the presenter on 8765, and raw TCP uses 8010. Do not repeat the historical one-port-one-connection claim. Source: RFC 9293 sections 2.2 and 3.1, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293 .'))
S.append(L.address('Read the address in parts.', notes='http is the application scheme; 127.0.0.1 means this laptop; 8009 selects the listening service; the application interprets /state. For a hostname, the resolver may use local configuration, cache or DNS. A numeric IP literal needs no DNS lookup. localhost normally resolves locally. A port is an endpoint number, not a permanent application name. The official presenter uses 8765; the later raw TCP observation uses 8010.'))
S.append(L.compare('Localhost means this computer.', ('This laptop', '**127.0.0.1**', 'The browser and server both run here.'), ('Another device', 'The server\'s reachable IP', 'localhost on a phone means that phone.'), notes='46-49 minutes, following the URL figure at 43-46. Trace scheme, host resolution, IP, TCP port, WebSocket handshake/path, then JSON messages. localhost on a phone refers to that phone, not the instructor laptop. Binding and client destination are different settings: 0.0.0.0 is a wildcard bind address, not the address to send to. The current app deliberately binds 127.0.0.1. A LAN extension needs explicit authorization and rehearsal of network reachability, firewall and access controls; do not simply expose the teaching service. Historical echo uses localhost:8765; its push variant binds 0.0.0.0:8765. Read those sources, but do not launch them alongside the presenter. Source: Python socket documentation https://docs.python.org/3/library/socket.html#socket-families and current pinned server.py.', conclusion='Our demo uses **127.0.0.1:8009**.'))
S.append(L.rows('Ask one diagnostic question at a time.', [('My address?', '**ip addr** on Linux; **ipconfig** on Windows.'), ('A listener?', '**ss -ltn** on Linux; **netstat -an** on Windows/macOS.'), ('An app reply?', '**curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8009/state**')], notes='49-52 minutes. These commands answer different questions: interface configuration, route selection, open/listening sockets, then an application response. ifconfig shows interface addresses, not the default gateway; on macOS use route -n get default for that. netstat exists on many systems but flags/output differ. On modern Linux ip/ss are often preferred; ifconfig/netstat may need legacy packages. Demonstrate only the commands available on the instructor host, not five installations. Look for 127.0.0.1:8009 in LISTEN before requesting /state. An IP configuration alone does not prove a process is listening. Sources: Microsoft ipconfig https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/ipconfig ; Microsoft netstat https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/netstat ; iproute2 https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/network/iproute2/iproute2.git/tree/man/man8 .', conclusion='Inspect configuration, then the listener, then the reply.'))
S.append(L.compare('Probe a port or inspect its traffic?', ('Nmap', 'Is the port reachable?', 'Sends probes to a chosen host and port.'), ('Wireshark', 'What crossed the network?', 'Shows captured packets and their contents.'), notes='52-54 minutes. The optional Nmap command targets only the instructor\'s own loopback listener; no campus or classmate scans. Scan another system only with explicit permission from its owner and within the authorized scope. This is not a required installation. Wireshark can show the handshake and protocol data, but encrypted HTTPS/WSS hides application payloads unless separately decrypted with authorization. Capture only the synthetic demo traffic, close unrelated apps, and do not publish credentials, cookies, student identifiers or raw captures. Use a prepared local capture if live capture is unavailable. Nmap sends probes while Wireshark primarily observes captured traffic. Sources: https://nmap.org/book/man.html ; https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsug_html_chunked/ChapterIntroduction.html .', conclusion='Use only your own prepared demo traffic.'))
S.append(L.compare('Did an application answer?', ('Wrong route', '**GET /statee**', 'HTTP 404: an app answered.'), ('Server stopped', '**GET /state**', 'Connection failure: no HTTP response.'), notes='54-56 minutes. Ask for a prediction before requesting /statee from the running server. Compare its 404 with a request to /state while no server is listening. An HTTP error is still a response from an application. A connection failure may have no HTTP response. Read the visible status and body rather than calling every problem an internet outage.', conclusion='Same host and port. Different failure boundaries.'))
S.append(L.rows('What does this observation tell you?', [('No listener', 'Nothing is listening on TCP **8009**.'), ('HTTP 404', 'An app answered, but the route was not found.'), ('Old screen', 'What would you check before trusting it?')], notes='56-60 minutes. Give pairs one minute to reason, then explain their evidence. C proves an HTTP-speaking application answered, though not necessarily the intended route. D proves an app processed and rejected the request. E calls for checking the data contract and service identity. F is ambiguous until we check connection status, freshness and data flow. B identifies a local socket issue, not necessarily a broken network. A may be irrelevant if the demo uses loopback. These are formative discussion prompts, not a new graded quiz or final-course quiz items. Students should justify the boundary instead of guessing a command.', conclusion='Explain the evidence before choosing the next check.'))
S.append(statement('Break / 10 minutes', size=96,
    notes='60-70 minutes. No tutorial implementation work belongs in this break. Take the full ten-minute break; no new material belongs in it.'))
S.append(L.steps('Three jobs in the journey', [('Find', '**DNS** looks up a name.'), ('Request', '**HTTP** asks an app for a result.'), ('Continue', '**WebSocket** carries later messages.')], notes='70-71 minutes. Explain the different jobs rather than a flat list of acronyms. A DNS resolver can return A/AAAA records, but DNS stores other kinds of records too. HTTPS provides encrypted, integrity-protected communication and authentication through TLS, not a guarantee that returned content is truthful. HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 normally use TCP; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP. WebRTC is a family of browser APIs/protocols for peer connections, often requiring signaling and sometimes relay servers, not just another spelling of WebSocket. Our small local control app uses HTTP and WebSocket, not WebRTC. Sources: RFC 1034 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1034 ; RFC 9110 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; RFC 6455 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455 ; W3C WebRTC https://www.w3.org/TR/webrtc/ . Ask which mechanism fetches a saved record and which carries a live video call.', conclusion='Follow the same message through each job.'))
S.append(L.lookup(notes='71-73 minutes. gallery.example is a reserved example name, not a service we ask students to visit. The resolver may first consult local configuration/cache before contacting DNS. Multiple answers can represent multiple interfaces or service endpoints. DNS can work while a server is stopped, and an IP literal can work while DNS is broken. Ask how using a numeric loopback URL changes the chain of possible failures. DNS maps names to several kinds of records, not only URLs to websites. Source: RFC 1034 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1034 . The visible mapping is illustrative. gallery.example is a reserved example name; 192.0.2.23 belongs to the documentation-only TEST-NET-1 range (RFC 5737), not an actual running course service.'))
S.append(L.compare('HTTPS protects data in transit.', ('http://', 'An unencrypted exchange', 'Our prepared loopback teaching example.'), ('https://', 'An encrypted exchange', 'TLS also helps authenticate the server.'), notes='73-76 minutes. Explain why the tiny loopback demo uses http but public services normally use https. Do not send credentials over the plain teaching connection or expose it as a production service. An HTTP connection need not close after every response. HTTPS is not a claim that the website is honest, that every app request succeeded, or that data was saved. The transport-version detail prevents treating every web request as TCP without exception; students need the functional distinction, not version trivia. Sources: RFC 9110 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; RFC 8446 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446 ; RFC 9114 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114 .', conclusion='A secure exchange still needs a result you can check.'))
S.append(L.compare('Shared controls or live audio and video?', ('WebSocket', 'App messages', 'Chat, a shared slider or a live dashboard.'), ('WebRTC', 'Real-time media and data', 'A call, camera stream or peer data channel.'), notes='76-80 minutes. A two-way connection alone does not make an app a video-call system. WebRTC includes APIs and protocols for media/data, connectivity checks and secure transport. Signaling is coordinated separately and may itself use WebSocket or HTTP; that does not make the media a WebSocket message. WebSocket is simpler for our small JSON control messages. Peer-to-peer paths are not guaranteed because networks and NAT may require a relay. Ask students to choose one for a collaborative slider versus a live remote video critique and justify the traffic needs. Sources: https://www.w3.org/TR/webrtc/ ; https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455 .', conclusion='Choose for the traffic your interaction needs.'))
S.append(L.steps('An API gives programs a way to ask.', [('Request', '**GET /state**'), ('Application', 'Read the shared scene.'), ('Response', 'Return its current position.')], notes='80-83 minutes. Reconnect with the Week 4 tide example and the historical API examples. An application programming interface is a boundary programs use; not every API is an HTTP API. For this lecture trace a network HTTP request into a Python handler, then, when applicable, a representation out to a browser. HEAD and 204 responses have no content; do not assume every response has a JSON body. See RFC 9110 section 6.4.1. A request may require authentication in a real service, but our loopback teaching app uses no account. Separate network reachability, HTTP status, parsed data and application meaning. Source for HTTP request/response semantics: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 .', conclusion='Reuse the Week 4 tide API: ask for data, then draw it.'))
S.append(L.diagram('One request, one corresponding response', F.request_response(), notes='83-86 minutes. Use this as a short optional live observation: start the prepared current server on 127.0.0.1:8009 and run curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8009/state. No installation or line-by-line Scene implementation in the lecture. Read the status, content type and JSON body; ask where the browser\'s drawing happens after it receives data. If the example is not ready, inspect the documented message shape and label the discussion as a trace, not a live run. This is a read, not a mutation; the detailed two-view demo belongs to the tutorial. Source: https://github.com/sd5913/pfad/blob/66067e191bd1c58b9c8d530dbc3fa8b50d69657a/week09/server.py .'))
S.append(L.address('REST: work with resources.', notes='86-89 minutes. These gallery URLs are illustrative, not endpoints in the supplied scene server. REST emphasizes resources, representations, a uniform interface and stateless requests; it is not a synonym for every JSON-over-HTTP service. Stateless requests do not mean the server cannot store art records. Replace the old invented UPDATE method with PUT/PATCH and distinguish their semantics. POST can have several application-defined meanings, not only create. GET should not request a mutation. Our small server implements GET /state and POST /action; do not advertise PUT/PATCH/DELETE routes it lacks or claim complete REST conformance. Sources: Fielding chapter 5 https://ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/rest_arch_style.htm ; RFC 9110 section 9 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; RFC 5789 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5789 .', remote=True))
S.append(L.compare('Read an artwork. Add an artwork.', ('GET /artworks/7', 'Read artwork 7', 'Ask for its data.'), ('POST /artworks', 'Submit a new artwork', 'Send data to the collection.'), notes='86-89 minutes. These gallery URLs are illustrative, not endpoints in the supplied scene server. REST emphasizes resources, representations, a uniform interface and stateless requests; it is not a synonym for every JSON-over-HTTP service. Stateless requests do not mean the server cannot store art records. Replace the old invented UPDATE method with PUT/PATCH and distinguish their semantics. POST can have several application-defined meanings, not only create. GET should not request a mutation. Our small server implements GET /state and POST /action; do not advertise PUT/PATCH/DELETE routes it lacks or claim complete REST conformance. Sources: Fielding chapter 5 https://ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/rest_arch_style.htm ; RFC 9110 section 9 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; RFC 5789 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5789 .', conclusion='These gallery routes are illustrative; the demo uses **GET /state**.'))
S.append(L.rows('Change a saved artwork.', [('PUT', '**/artworks/7**: replace its data.'), ('PATCH', '**/artworks/7**: change selected parts.'), ('DELETE', '**/artworks/7**: remove the resource.')], notes='86-89 minutes. These gallery URLs are illustrative, not endpoints in the supplied scene server. REST emphasizes resources, representations, a uniform interface and stateless requests; it is not a synonym for every JSON-over-HTTP service. Stateless requests do not mean the server cannot store art records. Replace the old invented UPDATE method with PUT/PATCH and distinguish their semantics. POST can have several application-defined meanings, not only create. GET should not request a mutation. Our small server implements GET /state and POST /action; do not advertise PUT/PATCH/DELETE routes it lacks or claim complete REST conformance. Sources: Fielding chapter 5 https://ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/rest_arch_style.htm ; RFC 9110 section 9 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; RFC 5789 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5789 .', conclusion='The **method** states the operation; the **path** identifies the resource.'))
S.append(L.rows('Read the HTTP status first.', [('2xx', 'The request succeeded at the HTTP level.'), ('4xx', 'The request needs attention, such as **404**.'), ('5xx', 'The server could not fulfill the request.')], notes='89-92 minutes. Status-code classes communicate the result of handling the request. A 200 response still needs interpretation against the application contract; it does not prove the screen updated or persistent storage committed. Use the actual x contract to predict 0, 100 and 101: the first two are accepted, 101 is rejected before mutation. Valid JSON alone does not establish a valid command. This is a rule/result explanation, not the Scene code walkthrough. Specific status choices for malformed JSON and invalid x belong to this server, not every possible framework. Link 404 and missing-listener observations back to the first half. Ask whether a received rejection proves the request never reached the server: no. Sources: RFC 9110 section 15 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; current server.py.', conclusion='Then inspect the returned data and what the app actually did.'))
S.append(L.compare('Validate before changing the scene.', ('0 to 100', 'Accepted', 'The position and revision can change.'), ('101', 'Rejected: HTTP 422', 'Position and revision stay unchanged.'), notes='89-92 minutes. Status-code classes communicate the result of handling the request. A 200 response still needs interpretation against the application contract; it does not prove the screen updated or persistent storage committed. Use the actual x contract to predict 0, 100 and 101: the first two are accepted, 101 is rejected before mutation. Valid JSON alone does not establish a valid command. This is a rule/result explanation, not the Scene code walkthrough. Specific status choices for malformed JSON and invalid x belong to this server, not every possible framework. Link 404 and missing-listener observations back to the first half. Ask whether a received rejection proves the request never reached the server: no. Sources: RFC 9110 section 15 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; current server.py.', conclusion='Valid JSON still has to meet the application\'s rules.', colors=(L.SERVER, L.ACTION)))
S.append(L.compare('What survives a process restart?', ('RAM', 'The current scene', 'This Python process forgets it when it stops.'), ('Saved data', 'Records on disk', 'The app can load them in a new process.'), notes='92-94 minutes. Correct the source-deck implication that HTTP/REST means no server-side data. The server can store resources while each request carries the context needed to handle it. Our Scene lives in one Python process and resets on restart, regardless of whether HTTP or WebSocket carries its messages. Persistence is a separate design choice, explored next week with related records. A continuing connection is not the same lifetime as the underlying data. Source: Fielding REST stateless constraint https://ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/rest_arch_style.htm .', conclusion='A **stateless request** can still read stored data.'))
S.append(L.steps('WebSocket keeps the conversation open.', [('Connect', 'Open one channel.'), ('Send', 'The client sends an action.'), ('Push', 'The server can send later updates.')], notes='94-95 minutes. Distinguish a persistent bidirectional protocol capability from one echo program\'s chosen sequential loop. WebSocket messages can carry text or binary data; JSON is our application choice. TCP ACK, WebSocket Ping/Pong and application acknowledgement are three different mechanisms. The next three excerpts make this distinction concrete without replacing the theory lecture with a framework tutorial. Source: RFC 6455 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455 .', conclusion='Either side can send. Each message has an application meaning.'))
S.append(L.code('The connection stays open between messages', ['async with connect("ws://localhost:8765") as websocket:', '    while True:', '        await websocket.send("Hello, world!")', '        response = await websocket.recv()', '        print(response)', '        await asyncio.sleep(1)'], notes=f'95-97 minutes. Actual excerpt from websocket_client_example.py. connect performs the WebSocket opening handshake over a TCP connection and the context keeps it open across loop iterations. This code chooses send then receive then wait, even though WebSocket supports messages in both directions independently. One recv returns one WebSocket message, not an arbitrary TCP byte fragment. Ask whether the server has to close the connection after each reply: no. Trace localhost resolution, TCP 8765, WebSocket, text payload. The historical source uses unpinned dependencies and is not our install route. Read it only; the current tested scene stays on 8009. Source: {ARCHIVE}/websocket_client_example.py ; protocol: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455 .', conclusion='**send()** sends one message. **recv()** waits for one message.', focus=(2, 3)))
S.append(L.code('Echo sends an application reply', ['async def echo(websocket):', '    async for message in websocket:', '        print(f"Received message: {message}")', '        await websocket.send(f"You sent: {message}")'], notes=f'97-99 minutes. Actual echo handler from websocket_server_echo.py. One incoming text message produces one outgoing text message. The surrounding source listens on localhost:8765, conflicting with our presenter, so do not run it in this lecture. Ask what the output is for Hello, world!: You sent: Hello, world! This reply is application data, not the TCP ACK or the WebSocket control Pong. The simple echo handler alone does not demonstrate unsolicited server push, shared state or broadcasting to other clients. Source: {ARCHIVE}/websocket_server_echo.py .', conclusion='**send()** replies to the message just received.', focus=(3,)))
S.append(L.code('The server can send without a new request', ['async def constant_ping(websocket):', '    while True:', '        await asyncio.sleep(5)', '        await websocket.send("Ping!")', '', '# Inside echo(websocket):', 'tasks = [respond(websocket), constant_ping(websocket)]', 'await asyncio.gather(*tasks)'], notes=f'99-103 minutes. Actual lines from websocket_server_echo_ping.py, with an explanatory comment between the two excerpts. echo() constructs tasks = [respond(websocket), constant_ping(websocket)] and gathers both coroutines. respond handles replies while constant_ping sends text every five seconds. The string Ping! is neither an RFC 6455 Ping control frame nor ICMP ping. The source listens on 0.0.0.0:8765; read it, do not expose it or start it beside the presenter. Predict the original client\'s next recv if Ping! arrives first: it may print the unsolicited message as though it were the reply to its send. A real app needs an independent receive path and message types/IDs. Our current browser does that for state, ack and rejected messages. Source: {ARCHIVE}/websocket_server_echo_ping.py ; control-frame distinction https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-5.5 .', conclusion='**"Ping!"** is app text, sent every **5 seconds**.', focus=(2, 3)))
S.append(L.diagram('One send is not one receive.', F.framing(), notes='103-106 minutes. Read the diagram; run the raw TCP example only in the tutorial extension. A buffer accumulates bytes until newline, then extracts a complete message. One read may contain multiple messages or part of one. TCP preserves byte order, not application send boundaries. WebSocket provides message framing before our server parses JSON. Ask what fails if code decodes each recv as one complete color. Framing, reliability and acknowledgement are separate jobs.'))
S.append(L.diagram('One scene, two views', F.journey(2), notes='106-109 minutes. Use the actual tutorial contract without walking through implementation: action {id:a-1, action:set_x, x:25}; state {type:state, x:25, revision:1}; acknowledgement {type:ack, id:a-1, revision:1}. Quote these verbally as fields rather than pretend these unquoted notes are literal JSON. Both clients can observe the same state but only the sender needs its correlated acknowledgement. Revisions order changes within this process lifetime; they are not wall-clock timestamps. The current Python broadcast loop sends separately to each connected WebSocket. The tutorial checks one Scene.apply boundary edit. Detailed await, lock and concurrency work remains optional reference. Source: https://github.com/sd5913/pfad/tree/66067e191bd1c58b9c8d530dbc3fa8b50d69657a/week09 .'))
S.append(L.compare('State and ack answer different questions.', ('State snapshot', 'What does the scene look like?', 'Every connected view can receive it.'), ('Acknowledgement', 'Was my action applied?', 'The reply names the sender\'s action ID.'), notes='106-109 minutes. Use the actual tutorial contract without walking through implementation: action {id:a-1, action:set_x, x:25}; state {type:state, x:25, revision:1}; acknowledgement {type:ack, id:a-1, revision:1}. Quote these verbally as fields rather than pretend these unquoted notes are literal JSON. Both clients can observe the same state but only the sender needs its correlated acknowledgement. Revisions order changes within this process lifetime; they are not wall-clock timestamps. The current Python broadcast loop sends separately to each connected WebSocket. The tutorial checks one Scene.apply boundary edit. Detailed await, lock and concurrency work remains optional reference. Source: https://github.com/sd5913/pfad/tree/66067e191bd1c58b9c8d530dbc3fa8b50d69657a/week09 .', conclusion='An action ID correlates a reply. It does not deduplicate retries.'))
S.append(L.diagram('The sender and observer know different things.', F.uncertainty(), notes='109-112 minutes. Explain the supplied diagram as a controlled tutorial scenario. As an optional tutorial reference, use Send, ignore replies, disconnect with A at 73. This deliberately suppresses the client observation; it is not evidence of random packet loss. B receives the real broadcast and displays 73. A says Unknown because it did not observe its own matching acknowledgement. The tutorial reconnects A and inspects without resending. A missing reply alone establishes neither success nor failure. A repeat set_x can retain the same position but advances revision; a repeated spend could charge twice. An action ID supports correlation, not deduplication, unless the app stores and checks completed IDs.'))
S.append(L.steps('Reconnect to see the current state.', [('Disconnect', 'A keeps its last known view.'), ('Change', 'B changes the shared scene.'), ('Reconnect', 'A receives a fresh snapshot.')], notes='112-114 minutes. Predict the tutorial sequence: disconnect A, change the scene in B, then reconnect A. Connect makes a new channel and the server sends its current snapshot. A visibly remains disconnected while B works. Reconnection does not resend the old action or establish exactly-once delivery. Callbacks from an old closed channel must not change the new channel state.', conclusion='Reconnecting reads state; it does not resend the old action.'))
S.append(L.rows('Choose for the interaction.', [('Spend a coin', 'Confirm the action; handle duplicate requests.'), ('Move an avatar', 'Prefer fresh positions to obsolete updates.'), ('Read a record', 'A request and response may be enough.')], notes='114-115 minutes. UDP can avoid retransmitting obsolete position updates, but delivery and order require application decisions. Ordered TCP bytes do not prove a game applied an event. Local-only is valid when one program owns the experience. These are design tradeoffs, not required group-project features.'))
S.append(L.rows('Name one useful boundary.', [('State', 'What should the other program observe?'), ('Command', 'What can it ask to change?'), ('Failure', 'What can the interface honestly confirm?')], notes='115-117 minutes. A local-only proposal can name an invalid-input or missing-file failure. Ask teams for one reason for their boundary and one observable check. Connect this to a usable increment, evidence and a next fix in the recommended GitHub Project or an equivalent accessible progress record. Networking, AI and a particular engine remain optional project choices.', conclusion='A local-only project is also a valid choice.'))
S.append(L.rows('Tutorial: try, observe, build.', [('30 min', '**WebSockets:** send, change one rule, reconnect.'), ('30 min', '**Multiboxes:** observe one shared physics world.'), ('40 min', '**Your project:** group work and TA feedback.')], notes=f'117-120 minutes. The separate two-hour tutorial allocates 0-10 setup/pair fallback, 10-40 WebSockets, 40-50 break, 50-80 Multiboxes observation and 80-120 group work and TA feedback. Start the small scene with uv run python server.py from week09, using port 8009. Trace one action and its state/ack, change the maximum to 80, restart and test 80/81, then restore 100. Disconnect/reconnect to receive current state. Multiboxes is a prepared demonstration or paired observation, not a second installation or code-edit exercise. Keep the final 40 minutes for a usable project increment, blockers and TA advice, including teams still getting started and unresolved opening AMA questions. Detailed await/lock, slow-client and uncertain-reply exercises remain optional references. No extra Canvas upload, login or grade. Core lecture revision covers network roles/layers, IP/routes/ports, delivery/transport, application protocols/APIs, framing and state/reply uncertainty. Version/flag memorization and optional LAN/OSC setup are outside core revision. This prepares concepts for the later final-course quiz; no end-of-lecture quiz is added. Setup and break each have ten minutes. Complete tutorial: {PRACTICAL}', conclusion=f'[Open the tutorial: setup + break + these three blocks]({PRACTICAL})'))
